  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two optical schemes for building multiphoton entangled states by combining simpler entangled-photon sources. On the left, a two-photon polarization-entanglement source is repeatedly connected through polarizing beam splitters, so that successive pairs are fused into a larger polarization-entangled GHZ state. The repeated layout and fading continuation indicate that this process can be extended to an arbitrary number of photons, producing an \\(N\\)-photon state from chained two-photon sources.\n\nOn the right, an analogous but more elaborate interferometric setup is shown for orbital angular momentum (OAM). A two-photon, three-dimensional OAM-entanglement source feeds a network of 50:50 beam splitters, with a trigger arm and mode-transforming elements including an OAM \\(=-2\\) hologram and dove prisms set at \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(90^\\circ\\). These components appear to manipulate and interfere spatial modes so that three photons emerge in a 3D OAM-GHZ state. Overall, the figure highlights a conceptual parallel: simple entangled pairs can be fused into larger GHZ-type states, first in polarization and then in a higher-dimensional OAM degree of freedom using interferometric mode control.",
